   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.4",
    "topic_title": "Membrane Permeability",
    "stem": "A researcher makes artificial vesicles enclosed by a pure phospholipid bilayer containing no proteins. Equal concentrations of four substances are added to the solution outside the vesicles. Which substance would enter the vesicles most rapidly, and what is the reason?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "H₂O, because it is smaller than every other substance listed and so passes through the bilayer fastest",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap judges permeability by size alone and ignores polarity. Water is small but polar, so the hydrophobic core lets it through only in small amounts without aquaporins, much more slowly than a small nonpolar molecule. Fix: Ask whether the molecule is polar or nonpolar before considering size, since a hydrophobic interior slows polar molecules."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Glucose, because cells require it for energy, so the membrane allows it to pass readily",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap explains movement by what the cell needs rather than by molecular properties. Glucose is a large polar molecule that cannot cross the hydrophobic core in meaningful amounts, and these vesicles lack the transport proteins it would require. Fix: Explain crossing using the molecule's size, polarity, and charge, and check whether a needed transport protein is present."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "O₂, because it is small and nonpolar, so it can dissolve through the hydrophobic interior of the bilayer without a protein",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The bilayer's hydrocarbon tails form a hydrophobic core that small nonpolar molecules such as O₂ pass through freely. Polar molecules and ions are blocked or slowed by that core, and these vesicles have no channels or carriers to help them, so O₂ crosses fastest."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "K⁺, because a single ion is tiny and can slip between the phospholipid molecules",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap treats a charged particle as if small size were enough. The charge makes K⁺ strongly hydrophilic, so the hydrocarbon tails block it, and with no channel proteins it essentially cannot cross. Fix: Check for a charge first; any ion needs a channel or transport protein to cross the hydrophobic interior."
     }
    ]
   },
